--- body ---
## Context

Reported three times by the author in one sitting, on v0.4.10 against his own
library (~13,000 attachments, Zotero 10.0.2), each time as a different symptom:

- "Scan starts at Index 0% and progresses slow."
- "The scan does not has an animation."
- "Seems Stuck at Preparing the next attachment..."

All three are one defect. The panel's document-status line is computed from
`s.active` and `s.pending` alone and never consults `s.phase`
(`plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js:2220`):

```js
const activeMessage = s.active === null
  ? (s.pending && s.pending.length > 0 ? sdtText('active-preparing') : sdtText('active-none'))
  : sdtText('active-file', { ... });
```

During a census neither input says what it looks like it says:

- `state.active` is assigned in exactly one place (`scheduler.js:226`, when a
  document is admitted for settling) and cleared in one (`:323`). The census
  loop never touches it, so `active === null` for the whole walk.
- `state.pending` still holds the PREVIOUS generation's queue, because
  `refreshQueue()` runs only after the census closes (`scheduler.js:170`). On
  the author's library that is 13,011, unchanged for the duration.

So the branch taken is `active-preparing` — "Preparing the next attachment…" —
held for the entire census, while no attachment is being prepared. Nothing
animates because no document is active. Coverage climbs from 0 because
`state.counts` is reset at census start (`scheduler.js:143`) and the held
`censusSnapshot` is only populated when a previous census had completed in this
session (`:142`).

The scheduler already carries a decided label for this state — `census:
'phase-census'` in `SDT_PHASE_LABELS`, "Census" — and the document line ignores
it.

This is the author's own rule biting the sitter: a background sitter must not
cause alarm. The panel states something false about what the add-on is doing,
and the author's reading of it was that the add-on was stuck. It is the worst
version of that failure, because the sitter is working correctly.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — the `activeMessage` computation, ~2220;
  the `SDT_PHASE_LABELS` table it should consult, ~960; the string table, ~396.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — the census loop (~137-172) that leaves
  `active` null and `pending` stale; `refreshQueue()` at ~170.

## Actions

1. Make the document-status line phase-aware: during `phase === 'census'` say
   what the census is doing, using the counters that do move (`scanned` /
   `total`), not a claim about an attachment.
2. Decide what the coverage bar should show during the first census of a
   session, when no `censusSnapshot` exists. Climbing from 0 is honest about the
   walk but reads as lost work to a user who indexed 14% yesterday. A
   determinate census bar plus a separate, held coverage figure is one option;
   the author rules the class.
3. Check the same blindness elsewhere in the panel: any other line whose text is
   derived from `active`/`pending` without consulting `phase`.

## Test

Red first, in `tests/`: drive the scheduler into `phase === 'census'` with
`active === null` and a non-empty stale `pending`, render the panel's document
line, and assert it does NOT read "Preparing the next attachment…". That
assertion fails against today's code, which is the point.

## Verification

- [ ] The red test above fails before the fix and passes after.
- [ ] On a real library, a reconciliation census shows census progress rather
      than "Preparing the next attachment…", confirmed by the author at the
      panel, not by a headless assertion.

## Invariants

- No new modal, toast or alert. This is a wording-and-source defect in an
  existing line; the author's ruling stands that a background sitter logs and
  defers rather than alarming.
- `state.active` keeps its current meaning — a document under settlement. Do
  not widen it to cover census items to make the line come out right.

## Exit criteria

- The panel does not claim an attachment is being prepared while a census is
  walking.
- The three symptoms the author reported are each accounted for, in the ticket
  log, as fixed or as deliberately left (action 2 may be ruled out of scope).
